**Summary: the single seat-counting source of truth is `members.status === "active"` in two places** — `member-repository.countActiveMembers` and `usage-repository.recomputeUsage`'s seats subquery — both already exclude anything not literally `"active"`, so if suspended members get status `"suspended"` (distinct from removal), seat/quota logic is likely already correct with no code change (just confirm `archivedAt` stays null for suspended-but-not-removed members so they still show up in member lists but not seat counts — check `member-repository.listMembers`, which does NOT filter by status by default unless `input.status` is passed, so suspended members remain visible in the members list — that's probably desired).

---

### 8. Notifications: fan-out, `notify()`, digest job

**`src/server/services/notification-service.ts`** (full contents above). Key points:
- `notify(orgId, kind, recipients, payload)` (lines 46-89) writes one row per recipient, skipping `recipientId === payload.actorId` and anyone with all channels muted. **It does not check member status at all** — if a suspended member is a `recipients` entry (e.g. still an issue watcher/assignee), they'd still get a notification row. You may want to filter recipients by active status here, or a `MemberSuspendedError` check upstream on issue-assignment prevents them from becoming assignees in the first place (see item 9).
- `registerFanOut()` (lines 180-244) wires: `issue.assigned` → notify assignee; `issue.overdue` → notify assignee; `comment.created` → notify watchers + mentioned users; `member.joined` → notify org owner. **No `member.removed` or `member.role_changed` subscription exists here currently** — so no notification fan-out precedent for member lifecycle events; you'd be creating a new pattern if you want `member.suspended` to notify anyone.

**`src/server/services/digest-service.ts`** (full contents above) — `buildDigest`, `listDigestRecipients`, `renderDigest`. No member-status filtering; recipients come from `preferenceRepo.listDigestSubscribers(orgId)` — worth checking that repo to see if it filters by active status (not read, but likely should if suspended members shouldn't receive digest emails).

---

### 9. Issue creation / assignment (for `MemberSuspendedError`)

**`src/server/services/issue-service.ts`** (full contents above). Relevant functions:
- `createIssue(actor, input)` (lines 42-81) — no assignee-status check at all today; `input` presumably can include an initial assignee via `issueRepo.insertIssue`. Need to check `CreateIssueInput` schema for an `assigneeId` field (in `src/schemas/issue.ts`, not fully read) and, if present, validate the target member is active before creating.
- `assignIssue(actor, input)` (lines 150-189) — this is the primary spot to add a suspended-member guard:
```ts
export async function assignIssue(actor: Actor, input: AssignIssueInput): Promise<Issue> {
  assertOrgScope(actor, input.orgId);
  const before = requireFound(await issueRepo.findIssueById(input.orgId, input.issueId), "Issue", input.issueId);
  assertCan(actor, "issue:assign", issueResource(before));
  assertNotArchived("Issue", before.id, before);
  const after = await issueRepo.setIssueAssignee(input.orgId, input.issueId, input.assigneeId);
  ...
}
```
To add suspension protection you'd insert, right after the permission checks and before `setIssueAssignee`, something like: look up the target member via `memberRepo.findMember(input.orgId, input.assigneeId)` and throw a new `MemberSuspendedError` (to be defined, likely in `_support.ts` alongside `NotFoundError`) if `member.status === "suspended"`. This mirrors the existing `assertNotArchived` guard-clause style.

---

### 10. @handle mention resolution, `comment.created` payload, mention notifications

- **Handle resolution / extraction**: `src/lib/mentions.ts` (full contents above) — `handleOf`, `extractMentions`, `resolveMentions(body, members)`. Pure functions; take a `readonly MemberWithUser[]` and don't know about status themselves — filtering must happen at the call site.
- **Call site building `comment.created` payload**: `src/server/services/comment-service.ts::createComment` (full contents above), specifically `resolveMentionedUsers` (lines 174-191):
```ts
async function resolveMentionedUsers(orgId: OrgId, body: string, clientProvided: readonly UserId[]): Promise<readonly UserId[]> {
  const members = await memberRepo.listMembers({ orgId, limit: MENTION_LOOKUP_LIMIT, cursor: null });
  const resolved = resolveMentions(body, members.items);
  const known = new Set(members.items.map((member) => member.userId));
  return [...new Set([...resolved, ...clientProvided.filter((id) => known.has(id))])];
}
```
**This lists ALL members regardless of status** (no `status` filter passed to `memberRepo.listMembers`), so a suspended member's handle currently still resolves and would still trigger a mention notification. This is a prime spot to filter — pass `status: "active"` in the `listMembers` call, or filter `members.items` by `member.status === "active"` before calling `resolveMentions`/building the `known` set.
- `comment.created` is emitted at lines 77-82 of the same file with `mentionedUserIds`.
- **Mention notifications created**: `src/server/services/notification-service.ts::registerFanOut`, subscription on `comment.created` (lines 203-229) — `if (payload.mentionedUserIds.length > 0) { await notify(payload.orgId, "comment_mention", payload.mentionedUserIds, {...}); }`.

---

### 11. Member type/model, role ranking, last-owner protection

`src/types/member.ts` (full contents):

```ts
export type Role = "owner" | "admin" | "member" | "viewer";
export const ROLES: readonly Role[] = ["owner", "admin", "member", "viewer"];
export const ROLE_RANK: Readonly<Record<Role, number>> = { owner: 4, admin: 3, member: 2, viewer: 1 };

export type MemberStatus = "active" | "invited" | "suspended";

export interface User extends Timestamps {
  readonly id: UserId; readonly email: string; readonly name: string;
  readonly avatarUrl: string | null; readonly timezone: string; readonly emailVerifiedAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
}

export interface Member extends Timestamps, TenantScoped, SoftDeletable {
  readonly id: MemberId;
  readonly userId: UserId;
  readonly role: Role;
  readonly status: MemberStatus;
  readonly invitedBy: UserId | null;
  readonly joinedAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
  readonly lastSeenAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
}

export interface MemberWithUser extends Member { readonly user: User; }

export interface Invitation extends Timestamps, TenantScoped {
  readonly id: InvitationId; readonly email: string; readonly role: Role;
  readonly invitedBy: UserId; readonly token: string; readonly expiresAt: IsoTimestamp;
  readonly acceptedAt: IsoTimestamp | null; readonly revokedAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
}

export interface Actor {
  readonly userId: UserId; readonly orgId: OrgId; readonly role: Role;
  readonly isPlatformStaff?: boolean;
}

export interface SessionPrincipal {
  readonly userId: UserId; readonly email: string; readonly activeOrgId: OrgId | null; readonly expiresAt: IsoTimestamp;
}

export function hasRoleAtLeast(role: Role, atLeast: Role): boolean {
  return ROLE_RANK[role] >= ROLE_RANK[atLeast];
}
```

**Last-owner protection**: `member-service.ts::assertLastOwnerRetained(orgId, memberId, nextRole)` (see item 1) — called from both `updateMemberRole` (before demoting) and `removeMember` (before removing, using `nextRole: "member"` as a stand-in for "no longer owner"). **You should call the same helper from a new `suspendMember` function**, since suspending an owner is functionally equivalent to demoting/removing them for availability purposes.

**Repeated `MemberStatus` re-emphasis**: `"suspended"` is already a declared status value, but it's currently only ever written by `memberRepo.archiveMember` (i.e., on **removal**, not on a "temporarily disable but keep membership" action). This is the crux of the design decision flagged in item 5.

---

### 12. Existing tests for member-service — `tests/services/member-service.test.ts` (full contents)

Path: `.../tests/services/member-service.test.ts` (full contents shown above in the earlier read). Structure/patterns to follow:
